# D27 accept-both, SELF-HOST resolver + runtime lanes: the canonical `^` move
# sigil immediately AFTER a FUNCTION TYPE whose return type is a NAMED (user)
# type (`Point(int) ^cb`).
#
# AXIS CELL: glyph = `^` · position = named param whose type is a function
# type · function-type RETURN kind = NAMED user type (the primitive sibling
# cell is `d27_sh_caret_fntype_spec_prim.gg`) · lanes = Rust gg (C + LLVM),
# self-host resolver, self-host runtime.
#
# WHY THIS IS ITS OWN CELL: the parser reaches the function-type speculation
# through a DIFFERENT predicate for a named return type than for a primitive
# one — the two accept-sets are separate pieces of code, so a fix applied to
# one does not cover the other. Sampling only the primitive cell would be
# exactly the "one value of a typed axis is an anecdote" trap (Core #12).
#
# RED-verified against the PRE-port self-host lane: this program failed to
# compile through the self-host driver while its `!` twin built and ran.
# Post-port the self-host lane compiles it and its output MATCHES Rust gg.
#
# No new `!` site is added for the control (`fmt_no_new_move_bang_in_migrated_corpora`
# in tests/lints.rs is shrink-only and at zero headroom); the durable guard
# for the glyph PAIR at this predicate is the `sh_parser_caret_predicate_siblings`
# lint in tests/lints.rs, which asserts every ported predicate names BOTH
# tokens.
#
# Expected stdout: 7

struct Point:
    int x

Point make(int v):
    return Point(v)

void apply(Point(int) ^cb):
    Point p = cb(7)
    print(p.x)

void main():
    apply(^make)
